1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:06,297
Part Four built the smoothed signal G_z from prime powers, logarithm
bounds, and weighted ramps.

2
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:10,129
We can now compute rational intervals for its values.

3
00:00:10,652 --> 00:00:12,955
But why is this the signal that matters?

4
00:00:13,477 --> 00:00:18,871
The Landau bridge connects an upper ceiling on this signal to the
location of zeta's zeros.

5
00:00:19,394 --> 00:00:24,703
This episode will unpack that sentence, starting with what a zero
looks like on a map.

6
00:00:25,226 --> 00:00:30,002
Then we will explain the windows, and the reason a ceiling can work
without a floor.

7
00:00:30,524 --> 00:00:34,718
We will check small pieces of the reasoning in ACL2 as we go.

8
00:00:35,241 --> 00:00:41,059
The full complex analytic theorem and these finite arithmetic
examples will keep their own labels.

9
00:00:41,582 --> 00:00:46,867
This is Part Five of Landau Windows ACL Two: why a ceiling controls
zeros.

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,067
A complex number gives us two real coordinates.

11
00:00:52,589 --> 00:00:55,838
The real part tells us how far to move horizontally.

12
00:00:56,361 --> 00:00:59,767
The imaginary part tells us how far to move vertically.

13
00:01:00,289 --> 00:01:03,332
We write the point as beta plus i times its height.

14
00:01:03,855 --> 00:01:07,770
The symbol i obeys the rule that i squared equals minus one.

15
00:01:08,292 --> 00:01:13,274
For the map in this episode, you can simply read the two coordinates
as an address.

16
00:01:13,797 --> 00:01:18,209
A complex function takes an address and returns another complex
number.

17
00:01:18,732 --> 00:01:23,592
A zero is an input address where the output has both coordinates
equal to zero.

18
00:01:24,115 --> 00:01:27,958
It is not a point where just the real part of the output vanishes.

19
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:33,983
The map will show the input addresses of zeros, rather than drawing
a three-dimensional surface.

20
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:40,064
The Riemann zeta function begins with a familiar-looking infinite
sum.

21
00:01:40,586 --> 00:01:47,108
When the real part of its input is greater than one, add one divided
by each positive integer raised to that input.

22
00:01:47,630 --> 00:01:51,109
This series does not define the function everywhere we need it.

23
00:01:51,632 --> 00:01:57,135
Complex analysis extends the function to the rest of the plane,
apart from its pole at one.

24
00:01:57,658 --> 00:02:00,652
That extension is called analytic continuation.

25
00:02:01,174 --> 00:02:06,217
The nontrivial zeros lie in the strip between real part zero and
real part one.

26
00:02:06,739 --> 00:02:11,648
The Riemann Hypothesis places all of those zeros on the vertical
line at one half.

27
00:02:12,171 --> 00:02:16,729
The yellow dots here are sample known zeros from the original
video's data.

28
00:02:17,252 --> 00:02:21,906
A finite collection of dots cannot establish a statement about every
zero.

29
00:02:22,429 --> 00:02:27,144
The vertical line is the target, and the entire critical strip is
the territory.

30
00:02:29,267 --> 00:02:33,352
The zero set has a reflection symmetry across the line at one half.

31
00:02:33,874 --> 00:02:39,850
If a nontrivial zero appeared to the left, a reflected zero would
appear to the right at the same height.

32
00:02:40,373 --> 00:02:43,621
The purple dots in this illustration are hypothetical.

33
00:02:44,144 --> 00:02:46,641
We have not discovered an off-line zero.

34
00:02:47,164 --> 00:02:51,733
The symmetry comes from the functional equation together with
complex conjugation.

35
00:02:52,256 --> 00:02:58,414
Therefore, excluding every zero to the right of one half also
excludes every zero to its left.

36
00:02:58,936 --> 00:03:02,536
The only possible horizontal position left is one half.

37
00:03:03,059 --> 00:03:05,908
This is the mirror step from the original video.

38
00:03:06,430 --> 00:03:13,194
The Landau step will give us a way to exclude a region on the right,
by controlling a real-valued signal instead.

39
00:03:15,333 --> 00:03:20,558
Recall that G_z is the smoothed prime-power sum after subtracting
its main term.

40
00:03:21,080 --> 00:03:24,147
It is a real number for each positive real input.

41
00:03:24,670 --> 00:03:29,445
A ceiling says that this number is less than or equal to some chosen
upper bound.

42
00:03:29,968 --> 00:03:33,035
Here the bound is C times x to the power theta.

43
00:03:33,558 --> 00:03:38,200
The constant C is positive, and theta lies strictly between zero and
one.

44
00:03:38,723 --> 00:03:42,408
The exponent determines the rate of growth allowed by the ceiling.

45
00:03:42,930 --> 00:03:48,955
At x equal to sixteen, x to the one-half power is four, while x to
the three-quarter power is eight.

46
00:03:49,477 --> 00:03:52,156
The multiplier C can raise either ceiling.

47
00:03:52,679 --> 00:04:00,921
This animation borrows the original video's ceiling plot, and is a
schematic illustration rather than computed values of G_z.

48
00:04:01,444 --> 00:04:05,250
A ceiling allows the signal to fall arbitrarily far below it.

49
00:04:05,773 --> 00:04:08,585
We are not assuming an absolute-value bound.

50
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:14,676
The ceiling does not have to hold at every sufficiently large input.

51
00:04:15,198 --> 00:04:18,653
The windowed theorem asks for long stretches where it holds.

52
00:04:19,176 --> 00:04:23,224
Start a stretch at Y, and end it at Y to the three-halves power.

53
00:04:23,747 --> 00:04:27,602
For Y equal to four, that gives the interval from four to eight.

54
00:04:28,124 --> 00:04:32,621
For Y equal to sixteen, the interval runs from sixteen to sixty-
four.

55
00:04:33,144 --> 00:04:37,641
For Y equal to sixty-four, it runs all the way to five hundred and
twelve.

56
00:04:38,164 --> 00:04:44,515
These are example addresses, not a claim that the actual signal
obeys the ceiling on these particular intervals.

57
00:04:45,038 --> 00:04:48,202
Notice that the window grows in width as it moves out.

58
00:04:48,724 --> 00:04:54,979
Every real point in a selected window must satisfy the ceiling,
including the points between any samples.

59
00:04:55,502 --> 00:04:59,295
A single successful sample is not a whole-window certificate.

60
00:05:01,433 --> 00:05:03,567
Now the important word: recurring.

61
00:05:04,089 --> 00:05:07,847
First fix the exponent theta and the positive constant C.

62
00:05:08,370 --> 00:05:11,473
Those two choices stay fixed across the windows.

63
00:05:11,995 --> 00:05:15,850
Someone can then demand any starting threshold, however large.

64
00:05:16,373 --> 00:05:21,270
Beyond that threshold, there must be some window on which the same
ceiling holds throughout.

65
00:05:21,792 --> 00:05:25,732
The window does not have to begin exactly at the demanded threshold.

66
00:05:26,255 --> 00:05:28,715
There may be gaps between the good windows.

67
00:05:29,238 --> 00:05:32,705
But there cannot be a final good window after which they stop.

68
00:05:33,227 --> 00:05:35,433
The order of these choices matters.

69
00:05:35,956 --> 00:05:40,405
Choosing a new and larger C for each window would be a different
hypothesis.

70
00:05:40,927 --> 00:05:44,636
We need arbitrarily large good windows for one fixed ceiling.

71
00:05:46,767 --> 00:05:50,039
Here is the theorem with its conditions gathered together.

72
00:05:50,562 --> 00:05:55,423
Fix theta strictly between zero and one, and fix a positive constant
C.

73
00:05:55,945 --> 00:06:04,661
Suppose the bound G_z of x is less than or equal to C times x to the
power theta holds on arbitrarily large windows of the stated shape.

74
00:06:05,183 --> 00:06:09,171
Then zeta has no zero whose real part is greater than theta.

75
00:06:09,694 --> 00:06:14,324
In the picture, every zero must stay on or to the left of the
boundary at theta.

76
00:06:14,847 --> 00:06:18,253
The repository proves this classical statement in Lean.

77
00:06:18,776 --> 00:06:22,327
This card shows the actual theorem statement from its source.

78
00:06:22,850 --> 00:06:29,989
The notation for a closed interval includes both endpoints, and the
conclusion says that the zeta value is not zero.

79
00:06:30,512 --> 00:06:35,942
The proof supplies the analytic connection; the ceiling itself
remains a hypothesis.

80
00:06:36,465 --> 00:06:40,053
Proving that hypothesis is a separate mathematical task.

81
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:44,988
We can also read the theorem by contradiction.

82
00:06:45,511 --> 00:06:50,129
Suppose there were a zero with horizontal coordinate beta greater
than theta.

83
00:06:50,652 --> 00:06:52,264
Fix a positive C.

84
00:06:52,786 --> 00:06:57,902
There could no longer be arbitrarily large windows on which the
corresponding ceiling holds.

85
00:06:58,424 --> 00:07:05,273
In fact, beyond some threshold, every window of that shape would
contain a point where the signal is strictly above the ceiling.

86
00:07:05,795 --> 00:07:10,208
This is the logical negation of having arbitrarily large good
windows.

87
00:07:10,730 --> 00:07:13,979
It does not mean the signal is above the ceiling everywhere.

88
00:07:14,502 --> 00:07:18,235
It does not say that every sampled point detects a violation.

89
00:07:18,758 --> 00:07:24,442
And this classical existence statement has not yet computed the
threshold or the violating address.

90
00:07:24,965 --> 00:07:30,141
Making that information explicit is why the arithmetic search and
its error budgets matter.

91
00:07:32,267 --> 00:07:36,861
To understand the proof's structure, it helps to change the
horizontal coordinate.

92
00:07:37,383 --> 00:07:41,795
Write x as e to the power u, so u is the natural logarithm of x.

93
00:07:42,318 --> 00:07:46,136
Multiplication of x now becomes addition in the new coordinate.

94
00:07:46,659 --> 00:07:54,223
The window from Y to Y to the three-halves power becomes the
interval from U to three-halves U, where U is the logarithm of Y.

95
00:07:54,745 --> 00:07:58,345
The power ceiling becomes C times e to the power theta u.

96
00:07:58,868 --> 00:08:03,850
Define the new signal g of u to mean G_z evaluated at e to the power
u.

97
00:08:04,373 --> 00:08:09,233
No new prime-counting statistic has appeared; we have changed its
address system.

98
00:08:09,756 --> 00:08:15,320
In logarithmic coordinates, the window has a width equal to one half
of its starting address.

99
00:08:15,842 --> 00:08:18,812
That shape will fit the proof's concentrated weights.

100
00:08:20,933 --> 00:08:24,703
The original video's waves give a useful first intuition.

101
00:08:25,226 --> 00:08:32,838
A hypothetical zero with real part beta suggests an oscillating
contribution with a growth scale like e to the power beta u.

102
00:08:33,361 --> 00:08:36,161
Its height supplies an oscillation frequency.

103
00:08:36,683 --> 00:08:40,271
Move beta to the right, and the envelope grows faster.

104
00:08:40,794 --> 00:08:45,679
A ceiling with exponent theta grows more slowly when beta is greater
than theta.

105
00:08:46,202 --> 00:08:49,935
This picture explains why a rightward zero should cause trouble.

106
00:08:50,458 --> 00:08:52,736
But the picture alone is not the proof.

107
00:08:53,259 --> 00:08:58,168
A cosine changes sign, and contributions from different zeros can
cancel.

108
00:08:58,691 --> 00:09:03,527
Seeing one wave above a curve in a finite animation does not settle
those issues.

109
00:09:04,050 --> 00:09:09,323
The rigorous argument uses a transform and estimates that can
control the combined signal.

110
00:09:11,467 --> 00:09:14,703
A transform takes many weighted readings of the signal.

111
00:09:15,226 --> 00:09:20,147
Here we integrate g of u against e to the power minus s u, over
nonnegative u.

112
00:09:20,670 --> 00:09:24,027
An integral is the continuous analogue of a weighted sum.

113
00:09:24,550 --> 00:09:31,386
The real part of s supplies exponential damping, while its imaginary
part supplies an oscillating phase.

114
00:09:31,909 --> 00:09:38,891
Far enough to the right, in the region with real part greater than
two, the crude growth bound makes this integral converge.

115
00:09:39,414 --> 00:09:43,741
Analytic continuation then connects the transform to a larger
region.

116
00:09:44,264 --> 00:09:48,276
The crucial identity ties it to the logarithmic derivative of zeta.

117
00:09:48,798 --> 00:09:53,077
Near a zero of zeta, that identity produces a pole of the transform.

118
00:09:53,600 --> 00:10:00,752
Locally a pole looks like a nonzero constant divided by s minus the
pole's address, plus a regular remainder.

119
00:10:01,274 --> 00:10:05,783
This is the precise bridge from a zero address to a feature of the
transform.

120
00:10:07,900 --> 00:10:11,548
Higher derivatives of the transform add powers of u to the weight.

121
00:10:12,071 --> 00:10:20,968
After normalization, the nonnegative kernel has the form u to the
power k, times e to the power minus sigma u, divided by k factorial.

122
00:10:21,491 --> 00:10:25,152
The factorial is the product of the positive integers through k.

123
00:10:25,674 --> 00:10:30,644
For positive k, this kernel has its peak at u equal to k divided by
sigma.

124
00:10:31,167 --> 00:10:35,021
Increasing k moves the region where the reading is most sensitive.

125
00:10:35,544 --> 00:10:42,417
The proof chooses the order in relation to the log-window, and
estimates the weighted mass both inside and outside it.

126
00:10:42,939 --> 00:10:47,582
Our curves show normalized kernel shapes so their different heights
fit on screen.

127
00:10:48,105 --> 00:10:52,214
Their absolute sizes are not being compared by this normalization.

128
00:10:52,736 --> 00:10:57,476
The actual estimates also include the signal's growth and the
ceiling exponent.

129
00:10:57,998 --> 00:11:02,277
The outside tails are bounded, rather than silently thrown away.

130
00:11:04,400 --> 00:11:08,764
The reason a one-sided estimate works starts with an elementary
identity.

131
00:11:09,286 --> 00:11:12,911
Take a real number x and add it to its absolute value.

132
00:11:13,433 --> 00:11:16,633
If x is positive, the result is twice x.

133
00:11:17,156 --> 00:11:21,883
If x is negative, its absolute value cancels it and the result is
zero.

134
00:11:22,406 --> 00:11:27,630
So the expression is twice the positive part of x, meaning the
larger of x and zero.

135
00:11:28,153 --> 00:11:36,221
For minus three the result is zero… for two the result is four… and
for minus one it is zero again.

136
00:11:36,744 --> 00:11:39,677
This is an identity about every real value.

137
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:45,133
Our ACL2 teaching function represents the positive part on rational
inputs.

138
00:11:45,656 --> 00:11:49,886
Its complete definition and the checked rational identity are shown
here.

139
00:11:50,409 --> 00:11:56,021
An upper bound on x, when that bound is nonnegative, also bounds its
positive part.

140
00:11:56,544 --> 00:11:59,465
No lower bound on x is needed for this step.

141
00:12:01,600 --> 00:12:05,939
Now use three signal values: minus three, two, and minus one.

142
00:12:06,462 --> 00:12:07,686
Give each weight one.

143
00:12:08,209 --> 00:12:11,324
The plain reading adds them, producing minus two.

144
00:12:11,847 --> 00:12:15,532
For a second reading, use phases one, minus one, and one.

145
00:12:16,055 --> 00:12:23,521
Multiplying by these phases produces minus three, minus two, and
minus one, so the signed reading is minus six.

146
00:12:24,044 --> 00:12:26,177
Its absolute value is six.

147
00:12:26,700 --> 00:12:30,518
Add back the plain reading, and six plus minus two is four.

148
00:12:31,041 --> 00:12:37,223
The triangle inequality says that the absolute value of a sum is at
most the sum of absolute values.

149
00:12:37,745 --> 00:12:44,909
Combining that fact with the positive-part identity bounds this
combined reading by twice the sum of the positive contributions.

150
00:12:45,432 --> 00:12:49,856
Here the only positive signal value was two, and twice two is four.

151
00:12:50,379 --> 00:12:56,148
If the first signal value falls from minus three to minus ten, the
combined reading is still four.

152
00:12:56,671 --> 00:13:02,150
The negative value disappeared from this upper bound; we did not
need to put a floor under it.

153
00:13:04,267 --> 00:13:08,206
Here are the two ACL2 functions used for this finite example.

154
00:13:08,729 --> 00:13:11,723
The plain function adds its three rational inputs.

155
00:13:12,245 --> 00:13:16,052
The signed function subtracts the middle input instead of adding it.

156
00:13:16,574 --> 00:13:22,683
These fixed real phases are a small special case, chosen so we can
see every calculation.

157
00:13:23,206 --> 00:13:27,994
The general teaching theorem allows all three rational signal values
to vary.

158
00:13:28,517 --> 00:13:34,298
It bounds the absolute signed reading plus the plain reading by
twice the sum of their positive parts.

159
00:13:34,821 --> 00:13:38,785
There is no nonnegativity hypothesis on the signal values.

160
00:13:39,308 --> 00:13:44,374
The project also has a disk-based version for complex phases and
nonnegative weights.

161
00:13:44,897 --> 00:13:51,891
For the continuous transform, the same idea controls the magnitude
of an oscillating reading plus a plain real reading.

162
00:13:52,414 --> 00:13:55,068
That extra real reading must be retained.

163
00:13:55,591 --> 00:14:02,633
Analytic estimates must control it before one can turn the combined
estimate into a bound on the oscillating reading alone.

164
00:14:04,767 --> 00:14:07,652
The other side of the argument comes from the pole.

165
00:14:08,174 --> 00:14:12,817
Expand the transform around an ordinary point to the right of the
forbidden region.

166
00:14:13,339 --> 00:14:18,503
Its successive coefficients record its higher derivatives, with
factorial normalization.

167
00:14:19,026 --> 00:14:27,353
For a simple pole at distance d, the pole contribution has
coefficient magnitudes proportional to one divided by successive
powers of d.

168
00:14:27,876 --> 00:14:34,700
A more distant regular remainder contributes a slower geometric
scale once an enclosing circle has been justified.

169
00:14:35,223 --> 00:14:41,744
In a favorable isolated picture, the zero is closer to our expansion
point than the boundary at theta.

170
00:14:42,267 --> 00:14:48,012
That smaller distance makes the pole's coefficient scale grow faster
relative to the ceiling scale.

171
00:14:48,535 --> 00:14:53,371
This diagram is a local schematic, not a certificate for an actual
zero.

172
00:14:53,894 --> 00:14:59,955
A complete proof must arrange the geometry and control every
competing pole and the regular remainder.

173
00:15:00,477 --> 00:15:03,592
Multiple zeros and clusters cannot be ignored.

174
00:15:04,115 --> 00:15:08,624
Those obligations will return when we build the pole and circle
certificates.

175
00:15:10,767 --> 00:15:16,027
After the analytic estimates are in place, the final contradiction
has an arithmetic shape.

176
00:15:16,550 --> 00:15:21,895
The same coefficient cannot be at least a lower estimate and at most
a smaller upper estimate.

177
00:15:22,418 --> 00:15:30,600
If one geometric scale grows by a factor greater than one relative
to the other, repeated multiplication eventually exceeds any fixed
target.

178
00:15:31,123 --> 00:15:34,783
For example, multiply repeatedly by three halves.

179
00:15:35,306 --> 00:15:47,245
The values at exponents zero, two, four, and six are one… nine
quarters… eighty-one sixteenths… and seven hundred twenty-nine
sixty-fourths.

180
00:15:47,767 --> 00:15:50,992
By the sixth exponent this example has passed eight.

181
00:15:51,514 --> 00:15:54,460
This demonstrates the geometric-growth mechanism.

182
00:15:54,983 --> 00:15:58,122
It does not derive the analytic estimates that feed it.

183
00:15:58,645 --> 00:16:05,008
We still need justified coefficients, remainders, and constants
before applying this mechanism to the transform.

184
00:16:07,133 --> 00:16:11,400
An existence argument says that some sufficiently large order will
work.

185
00:16:11,923 --> 00:16:15,547
The arithmetic version asks us to compute a sufficient order.

186
00:16:16,070 --> 00:16:23,379
The repository's geometric index does this for a rational growth
factor greater than one and a nonnegative rational target.

187
00:16:23,902 --> 00:16:27,853
Its definition uses the precision fuel introduced in Part Four.

188
00:16:28,376 --> 00:16:32,812
For growth factor three halves and target eight, it returns
seventeen.

189
00:16:33,335 --> 00:16:38,062
That is a conservative bound, not the first crossing we saw in the
example.

190
00:16:38,585 --> 00:16:47,967
The proof uses a linear lower estimate for repeated multiplication:
the kth power is at least one plus k times the quantity growth
factor minus one.

191
00:16:48,489 --> 00:16:53,629
At order seventeen, that lower estimate is nineteen halves, already
greater than eight.

192
00:16:54,152 --> 00:16:59,376
The checked theorem guarantees that every natural order at least
seventeen reaches the target.

193
00:16:59,898 --> 00:17:02,844
This is a real computed component of the project.

194
00:17:03,367 --> 00:17:08,264
It still needs the analytic data and inequalities supplied to the
larger argument.

195
00:17:10,400 --> 00:17:12,436
Return to the line at one half.

196
00:17:12,959 --> 00:17:18,874
Suppose the required recurring-window ceilings were available for
every exponent just above one half.

197
00:17:19,397 --> 00:17:26,621
For each such exponent, its positive constant may be different, but
it must stay fixed across that exponent's windows.

198
00:17:27,144 --> 00:17:32,380
A hypothetical zero with real part beta greater than one half leaves
a positive gap.

199
00:17:32,903 --> 00:17:35,267
Choose epsilon to be half that gap.

200
00:17:35,789 --> 00:17:40,808
The exponent one half plus epsilon then lies strictly between one
half and beta.

201
00:17:41,330 --> 00:17:45,136
The Landau theorem at that exponent excludes the supposed zero.

202
00:17:45,659 --> 00:17:49,392
For a concrete illustration, take beta equal to three quarters.

203
00:17:49,915 --> 00:17:53,709
Epsilon is one eighth, and the chosen exponent is five eighths.

204
00:17:54,232 --> 00:18:00,098
The ACL2 card checks this midpoint inequality for any rational beta
in the stated range.

205
00:18:00,621 --> 00:18:07,845
That arithmetic fact chooses the exponent in our example; the zero-
free conclusion still uses the analytic theorem.

206
00:18:08,368 --> 00:18:15,011
Excluding every zero on the right, together with reflection, puts
every nontrivial zero on the central line.

207
00:18:15,533 --> 00:18:22,903
This explains what the ceiling-building part of an RH proof has to
deliver; this episode has not constructed those ceilings.

208
00:18:25,033 --> 00:18:28,524
We can now name the job of this repository more clearly.

209
00:18:29,047 --> 00:18:33,762
The classical Landau theorem turns recurring ceilings into a zero-
free region.

210
00:18:34,285 --> 00:18:40,806
The arithmetic project aims to replace the relevant existence steps
with explicit constructions and checked bounds.

211
00:18:41,329 --> 00:18:48,759
The finite search, rational signal evaluator, one-sided estimates,
and computed orders each address part of that job.

212
00:18:49,282 --> 00:18:53,536
A proof of an isolated arithmetic lemma is not yet the full
reduction.

213
00:18:54,059 --> 00:18:59,998
The analytic meanings, zero data, remainder estimates, and final
composition must all match.

214
00:19:00,521 --> 00:19:05,733
Working toward Peano Arithmetic means accounting for those links as
well as running a checker.

215
00:19:06,256 --> 00:19:12,050
There is no claim that this is the only part of an RH argument that
could be brought into Peano Arithmetic.

216
00:19:12,573 --> 00:19:16,682
Nor have we ruled out the possibility of an entire RH proof there.

217
00:19:17,205 --> 00:19:22,562
Here we are making one useful proof component explicit and examining
exactly what it needs.

218
00:19:24,700 --> 00:19:26,955
The central idea is now in view.

219
00:19:27,477 --> 00:19:33,138
A long window limits the positive part of the signal where a
carefully chosen reading concentrates.

220
00:19:33,661 --> 00:19:39,600
The one-sided identity keeps large negative signal values from
spoiling that combined upper bound.

221
00:19:40,123 --> 00:19:43,541
A nearby pole supplies a competing coefficient scale.

222
00:19:44,064 --> 00:19:51,433
Once all the geometric and tail estimates are justified, those
scales become incompatible at a sufficiently large order.

223
00:19:51,956 --> 00:19:57,859
The arithmetic challenge is to make that order, the bounds, and the
resulting witness construction explicit.

224
00:19:58,382 --> 00:20:03,267
Next we will build the exponentially damped kernels and their finite
approximations.

225
00:20:03,789 --> 00:20:09,292
That is how the proof's weighted readings begin to become
computations with rational error budgets.

226
00:20:09,815 --> 00:20:13,536
The bridge tells us why those computations are worth constructing.
